  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎸 Blues Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Howlin', ramblin', big and little: names for blues singers, bands and records."
    },
    "intro": "Good blues names sound like a nickname someone earned: a place, a size, a howl or a fish, put in front of a real name, like <em>Little Irma Varnado</em> or <em>Tupelo Cleveland Riddick</em>. Half the great bluesmen and blueswomen were known that way. This **blues name generator** makes bluesman and blueswoman names in those classic shapes, plus blues band names and album titles.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good blues names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, for singers, bands and records:</p><ul><li><strong>Tupelo Cleveland Riddick</strong>: a hometown in front of the name, the way Memphis Minnie and Mississippi John Hurt were billed.</li><li><strong>Little Irma Varnado</strong>: \"Little\" is one of the oldest blues nicknames, as in Little Walter.</li><li><strong>Big Mama Fortune</strong>: for a singer with a voice to fill a juke joint.</li><li><strong>Willard \"Gravy\" Puckett</strong>: a nickname in quotation marks, which is how record labels often printed them.</li><li><strong>Hummin' Lemuel Glover</strong>: a nickname from how he sings, with the dropped g of the songs themselves.</li><li><strong>Earnest Tillman & the Kingsnakes</strong>: the leader's name and a band of hard-sounding animals, Chicago style.</li><li><strong>The Barrelhouse Hounds</strong>: a barrelhouse was a rough bar, and gave its name to a rolling piano style.</li><li><strong>Porch Light Blues</strong>: an album title in the oldest blues pattern of all: a thing, then \"Blues\".</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How blues singers got their names",
        "content": "<p>Blues names were mostly given, not chosen. They came from friends, audiences and record companies, and they fall into a few clear types:</p><ul><li><strong>Size and age:</strong> Big Bill Broonzy, Little Walter, Big Mama Thornton. \"Little\" and \"Big\" often had nothing to do with height.</li><li><strong>Places:</strong> Memphis Minnie, Mississippi John Hurt, Texas Alexander. A hometown told listeners what kind of blues to expect.</li><li><strong>Sound and manner:</strong> Howlin' Wolf (Chester Burnett), Lightnin' Hopkins (Sam Hopkins). Several early singers were billed as \"Blind\", like Blind Lemon Jefferson and Blind Willie McTell.</li><li><strong>Childhood nicknames:</strong> McKinley Morganfield became Muddy Waters. B.B. King's initials stand for \"Blues Boy\", from his days as the Beale Street Blues Boy on Memphis radio.</li><li><strong>\"Ma\" and \"Queen\"</strong> for the great women singers of the 1920s, like Ma Rainey.</li></ul><p>Choose <strong>Blues name</strong> for a singer in those shapes, <strong>Band</strong> for a leader and their backing group or a juke-joint band like <em>The Fish Fry Band</em>, and <strong>Album</strong> for titles like <em>Mean Old Flood</em> and <em>Down at the Blue Moon Lounge</em>. Use the gender filter for a bluesman or a blueswoman.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using a blues name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Keep your real surname.</strong> Most blues nicknames sit in front of a real name, so <em>Steady Sylvester Dancy</em> still sounds like a person.</li><li><strong>Name the album like a song.</strong> Blues records are often named after one track, and blues songs after one plain thing: a river, a landlord, an empty pocket. W.C. Handy's \"St. Louis Blues\" (1914) set the pattern.</li><li><strong>Check before you gig.</strong> Search the name on streaming services and with local venues before you print anything.</li></ul><p>Blues runs straight into the <a href=\"jazz-name-generator.html\">Jazz</a> and <a href=\"soul-singer-name-generator.html\">Soul Singer</a> name generators. For a band in any style, try the <a href=\"band-name-generator.html\">Band Name Generator</a>, and for the songs themselves the <a href=\"song-title-generator.html\">Song Title Generator</a>.</p><p>For more genres, see all the <a href=\"../categories/music_genres-names.html\">music genre name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is a good blues name?",
        "answer": "A nickname in front of a real name: a place (Tupelo Cleveland Riddick), a size (Little Irma Varnado), a sound (Hummin' Lemuel Glover) or a nickname in quotation marks (Willard \"Gravy\" Puckett). That's how the great blues singers were billed."
      },
